{
 "id": "2020-tablada-productive",
 "name": "Productive Facades Survey",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the expert web survey on productive facades (PF) for Singapore public housing (HDB) blocks: facades that combine tilted photovoltaic (PV) shading modules with vertical-farming (VF) planters for vegetables or ornamental plants. Participants rate rendered design variants on the paper's five-point scale (very poor, poor, fair, good, very good) in nine blocks: the exterior facade (clean, plants, PV, PV plus plants), the view from inside, PV module position seen from inside, planter position seen from inside, PV module arrangement, safety grill design, ease of operation and functionality, and the overall architectural quality of five window-facade and five balcony-facade designs. They first answer six background questions (gender, profession, industry sector, years of professional experience, experience in PV building integration, experience in farming, green facades or horticulture) and may comment at the end. Rebuilt from Tablada, Kosoric, Huang, Lau & Shabunko (2020), Architectural quality of the productive facades integrating photovoltaic and vertical farming systems: Survey among experts in Singapore, Frontiers of Architectural Research 9(2): 301-318, Section 2.1 and Table 1, with the stimuli from its Figs. 3-11 (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0; figures unchanged, the result chart printed above each set of designs is hidden by cropping the view, so participants see only the designs and their captions). The question wording is the paper's, as printed in each figure title. Image ids: exterior, view_inside, pv_position, plants_position, pv_arrangement, safety_grill, operation, quality_wf and quality_bf; the answer rating (1 = very poor to 5 = very good) is keyed <image id>_d<design number>, e.g. exterior_d4. Analysis as in the paper: share of each grade and the mean grade per design, compared between architects and designers and other professionals and between those with and without PV or horticulture experience. The paper (97 building professionals, 79% architects) found the exterior facade with PV and plants rated best (3.54 against 2.23 for the clean facade), plants seen from inside raised view ratings (3.90) while visible PV lowered them (2.84), one PV module per window or balcony beat two or three smaller ones, and architects and designers gave lower grades to designs with PV. Differences from the original study: (1) question 6 (material, texture and colour of PV modules and planters) is left out because its images are not printed; (2) the paper's introductory section had 15 questions, of which only the six demographic items are known from Table 1, so the other nine are left out, and the wording and answer options of these six follow Table 1 but the question text is ours; (3) the paper allowed a comment after each question; here one comment is asked at the end; (4) blocks appear in the paper's order and the introduction and consent text are ours.",
 "author": "Tablada, Kosorić, Huang, Lau & Shabunko",
 "year": "2020",
 "category": "Academic Research",
